{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"incident_l1_2","type":"text","text":"L1 incident\n\nQuote: \"i give the example... then i give them another one for them to do on their own\"\n\nOpen code: worked example then independent task\n\nInterpretation: guided-to-independent progression.","x":-2760,"y":-15,"width":360,"height":165},
		{"id":"coding_l1","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/02_first_cycle_coding/coding_L1C.md","x":-2470,"y":542,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"transcript_l1","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/01_raw_transcripts/L1_transcript.md","x":-2470,"y":745,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"memo_l1m2","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L1M2.md","x":-2460,"y":335,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"code_l1_example_based","type":"text","text":"## Code cluster: example-based procedural teaching\n\nCodes:\n- example-based teaching\n- step-by-step modelling demonstration\n- worked example then independent task\n- counterexample-based support\n- rationale-based feedback","x":-2400,"y":-360,"width":390,"height":190},
		{"id":"incident_l1_1","type":"text","text":"L1 incident\n\nQuote: \"Its by example\"\n\nOpen code: example-based teaching\n\nInterpretation: lecturer frames examples as the basic scaffolding mechanism.","x":-2205,"y":-15,"width":360,"height":145},
		{"id":"incident_l2_2","type":"text","text":"L2 incident\n\nQuote: \"Some show autonomy and do the work. Others expect me to solve the exercise first.\"\n\nOpen code: uneven student autonomy\n\nInterpretation: examples can support action, but can also become expected before starting.","x":-1720,"y":298,"width":360,"height":175},
		{"id":"memo_l2m3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L2M3.md","x":-1700,"y":705,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"coding_l2","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/02_first_cycle_coding/coding_L2C.md","x":-1420,"y":1045,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"transcript_l2","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/01_raw_transcripts/L2_transcript.md","x":-1420,"y":1225,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"incident_l2_1","type":"text","text":"L2 incident\n\nQuote: \"first I show an example\"\n\nOpen code: example-first teaching\n\nInterpretation: examples introduce the modelling task before students work independently.","x":-1145,"y":298,"width":360,"height":145},
		{"id":"memo_l2m2","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L2M2.md","x":-1125,"y":705,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"code_l2_dependence","type":"text","text":"## Code cluster: example-seeking and uneven autonomy\n\nCodes:\n- example-first teaching\n- autonomy-first exercises\n- uneven student autonomy\n- example-seeking when stuck\n- no universal checklist","x":-1460,"y":-55,"width":390,"height":175},
		{"id":"code_student_reasoning","type":"text","text":"## Cross-case code cluster: examples expose reasoning, not only shapes\n\nCases: S2, S4, L1, S6\n\nCodes:\n- learning from completed examples\n- example-based relation decoding\n- worked examples teach reasoning not shapes\n- rationale for relation choice\n- examples connect quality attributes to design decisions","x":-495,"y":215,"width":430,"height":240},
		{"id":"memo_s4m3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S4M3.md","x":-420,"y":900,"width":320,"height":80},
		{"id":"coding_s4","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/02_first_cycle_coding/coding_S4C.md","x":-420,"y":1100,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"transcript_s4","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/01_raw_transcripts/S4_transcript.md","x":-420,"y":1260,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"incident_s3_2","type":"text","text":"S3 incident\n\nQuote: \"The theoretical slides do not always include examples, or they include an example that does not show the full syntax\"\n\nOpen code: incomplete examples in theory slides\n\nInterpretation: partial examples do not provide enough validation for independent practice.","x":355,"y":358,"width":390,"height":195},
		{"id":"negative_risks","type":"text","text":"## Risks and negative cases\n\nExamples can create:\n- surface imitation\n- dependence on lecturer solution\n- false certainty that there is one correct diagram\n- weak transfer across domains or modelling families\n- insufficient support when examples are partial","x":495,"y":-130,"width":430,"height":210},
		{"id":"memo_s3m5","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S3M5.md","x":635,"y":775,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"coding_s3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/02_first_cycle_coding/coding_S3C.md","x":635,"y":1035,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"transcript_s3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/01_raw_transcripts/S3_transcript.md","x":635,"y":1255,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"incident_s3_1","type":"text","text":"S3 incident\n\nQuote: \"I had to wait until the professor gave an example\"\n\nOpen code: needed worked example before starting in crash course\n\nInterpretation: examples may be necessary in unfamiliar contexts, but also indicate authority dependence.","x":835,"y":363,"width":390,"height":185},
		{"id":"next_checks","type":"text","text":"## Evidence checks still needed\n\n1. Add excerpt-level IDs for all selected incidents.\n2. Distinguish lecturer interpretation from student direct evidence.\n3. Check whether examples improved later work or only felt helpful.\n4. Compare complete, partial, contrastive, and faded examples.\n5. Avoid verbatim use of reconstructed S4/S6 quotes unless source status is made explicit.","x":1115,"y":-160,"width":470,"height":270},
		{"id":"topic","type":"text","text":"# Evidence map: worked examples\n\nQuestion: how do worked examples support or limit modelling learning?\n\nUse this canvas as a traceability view: theme -> codes -> incidents/quotes -> source files.","x":-160,"y":-1760,"width":380,"height":170},
		{"id":"claim","type":"text","text":"## Provisional claim\n\nWorked examples help when they expose modelling reasoning, alternatives, criteria, and progression toward independent work.\n\nThey are weaker when they only show a final diagram or become templates for copying.","x":495,"y":-1760,"width":430,"height":180},
		{"id":"comparison","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/04_cross_case_analysis/comparison_worked_examples.md","x":1265,"y":-1710,"width":390,"height":80},
		{"id":"claim_progression","type":"text","text":"## Claim: examples scaffold first moves and progression\n\nSupported across L1, L2, L3, S1, and S3.\n\nExamples help learners move from text or unfamiliar notation into action when they are paired with staged practice, fading, or feedback. The same support can become dependence if learners wait for the example before attempting anything.","x":-1840,"y":-1035,"width":470,"height":250},
		{"id":"claim_reasoning","type":"text","text":"## Claim: examples need visible rationale\n\nSupported across L1, S2, S4, and S6.\n\nThe transferable part is not the final diagram shape. It is the explanation of why a relation, abstraction, quality refinement, or modelling choice fits the case.","x":-520,"y":-1035,"width":450,"height":220},
		{"id":"claim_granularity_multi","type":"text","text":"## Claim: examples calibrate granularity and stopping rules\n\nSupported across S4, S5, S6, and L4.\n\nExamples are useful for deciding what is enough, what is too detailed, what can be omitted, and how actor, goal, sequence, or quality-detail boundaries should be drawn.","x":2990,"y":-1035,"width":470,"height":240},
		{"id":"claim_risks_multi","type":"text","text":"## Claim: examples carry dependence and imitation risks\n\nSupported across L2, S2, S3, S4, and L3.\n\nExamples can lead to waiting, copying, false certainty, or weak transfer when they present a solution without criteria, alternatives, or changed-context practice.","x":630,"y":-1035,"width":460,"height":230},
		{"id":"memo_s1m5","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S1M5.md","x":-2105,"y":-575,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"memo_l3m4","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L3M4.md","x":-1780,"y":-575,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"memo_s2m3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S2M3.md","x":-170,"y":-575,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"memo_l4m6","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_L4M6.md","x":3310,"y":-575,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"incident_s5_2","type":"text","text":"S5 incident\n\nQuote: \"one bloated and one minimal\"\n\nOpen code: contrastive examples teach simplification\n\nInterpretation: comparing alternatives can transfer to later project modelling.","x":3680,"y":28,"width":380,"height":150},
		{"id":"code_granularity","type":"text","text":"## Cross-case code cluster: examples calibrate granularity\n\nCases: S4, S5, S6, L4\n\nCodes:\n- boundary between too much and too little detail\n- examples calibrate granularity\n- over-modelling tendency\n- contrastive examples teach simplification\n- examples calibrate actor and goal granularity\n- granularity and stopping problem","x":3900,"y":-352,"width":430,"height":265},
		{"id":"incident_s5_1","type":"text","text":"S5 incident\n\nQuote: \"Examples help me calibrate the level of detail\"\n\nOpen code: examples calibrate granularity\n\nInterpretation: examples support abstraction and stopping rules.","x":4140,"y":28,"width":380,"height":150},
		{"id":"incident_s6_1","type":"text","text":"S6 incident\n\nQuote: \"examples helped me understand acceptable abstraction: how many actors are enough, and when to stop refining goals\"\n\nOpen code: examples calibrate actor and goal granularity\n\nInterpretation: examples support judgement about boundaries.\n\nCaution: reconstructed transcript.","x":1830,"y":315,"width":410,"height":210},
		{"id":"code_traceability","type":"text","text":"## Code cluster: examples connect model families and rationale\n\nCodes:\n- examples connect quality attributes to design decisions\n- examples transform bullet qualities into modelled softgoals\n- continuous traceable example across model families\n- partial slides insufficient for NFR learning","x":2305,"y":-140,"width":410,"height":210},
		{"id":"incident_s6_3","type":"text","text":"S6 incident\n\nQuote: \"one continuous example from goals to NFR decisions to UML, with trace links documented\"\n\nOpen code: continuous traceable example across model families\n\nInterpretation: students need examples of translation paths, not isolated diagram fragments.\n\nCaution: reconstructed transcript.","x":2305,"y":485,"width":410,"height":230},
		{"id":"memo_s6m5","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S6M5.md","x":2680,"y":945,"width":320,"height":70},
		{"id":"incident_s6_2","type":"text","text":"S6 incident\n\nQuote: \"Without examples, I would have listed qualities as bullet points rather than modelling them\"\n\nOpen code: examples transform bullet qualities into modelled softgoals\n\nInterpretation: examples convert abstract concepts into modelled representations.\n\nCaution: reconstructed transcript.","x":2795,"y":315,"width":410,"height":220},
		{"id":"incident_s4_2","type":"text","text":"S4 incident\n\nQuote: \"why one relation is composition and another is just association\"\n\nOpen code: rationale for relation choice\n\nInterpretation: examples help with semantic judgement, not only notation recognition.\n\nCaution: reconstructed transcript.","x":-720,"y":550,"width":380,"height":190},
		{"id":"incident_s4_1","type":"text","text":"S4 incident\n\nQuote: \"It helps most with the reasoning. It is not just about memorising shapes\"\n\nOpen code: worked examples teach reasoning not shapes\n\nInterpretation: final diagrams are insufficient without decision rationale.\n\nCaution: reconstructed transcript.","x":-255,"y":553,"width":380,"height":187},
		{"id":"coding_s6","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/02_first_cycle_coding/coding_S6C.md","x":2385,"y":1200,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"transcript_s6","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/01_raw_transcripts/S6_transcript.md","x":2385,"y":1410,"width":330,"height":70},
		{"id":"memo_s6m3","type":"file","file":"qualitative_analysis/03_memos/memo_S6M3.md","x":2065,"y":945,"width":320,"height":70},
